  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure across the area will provide mostly dry \nconditions to the region. There will be some slight chances for \ndiurnal showers and thunderstorms which will be isolated in \nnature for Today and Wednesday although this should be confined \nto the northern tiers of our County Warning Area. According to \nthe National Blend of Models Wednesday looks to be dry although \none cannot rule out a diurnal shower or thunderstorm activity \nduring the maximum daytime heating as the area gets to \nanomalously high temperatures for this time of year. Confidence \non PoPs is low as central guidance is having a hard time dealing\nwith this type of solution and setup with a dirty high pressure\nsystem influencing the area. Had to increase PoPs for Thursday \nand thunderstorm coverage as guidance did not support much \nactivity as well and we are in a slight risk for Severe \nthunderstorms for much of the area. \n\nAlso, the moisture flux from the Great Lakes driving around the \nperiphery of the high pressure system will traverse into the \narea which will bring higher dew points, therefore humid \nconditions along with the heat will be the main theme through \nThursday and even into Friday. A convective complex is forecast \nby convective allowing models for Wednesday morning and \nafternoon, however timing and position of this feature is not \ncertain and could very well touch our northern most parts of \nthe area. SPC has a marginal threat for severe thunderstorms \njust touching our northern border. Guidance did not include much\nin the away of PoPs but since confidence is low decided to \naccept guidance this time around due to model uncertainty. \n\nElsewhere will be mainly dry and, for much of the lowlands, hot \nenough to maintain the Heat Advisories through at least Wednesday. \nFor Thursday, advisories or warnings will likely be needed as \nheat index values reach the 105 degree threshold of a warning. \nWill continue to monitor and issue as needed. The highest \nvalues will be centered across the Huntington Tri-state and \nCharleston Metro areas. Boundary layer temperatures will \nsupport these high temperatures until Friday when cold frontal \npassage is forecast later in the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFor Thursday, shower potential rises as a cold front swings \ndown from the south and promotes shower and thunderstorm \nactivity out ahead of the frontal boundary. SPC has us in a \nslight risk for possible severe thunderstorm potential with \nmodest shear and instability to support this as well as \nanomalously high temperatures that will add to the unstable \nenvironment.\n \nFor Friday, the cold frontal boundary slides through promoting \nmore chances for thunderstorm activity as well as severe \npotential as SPC has us in a slight risk (15% probability) for \nthe afternoon and evening. Models have the aforementioned feature\nslowing down, therefore it will likely affect Saturday as well \nduring the morning and afternoon hours. \n\nModels have surface high pressure building in behind the front \nwhich should suppress much in the way of shower or thunderstorm\npotential the rest of Saturday and Sunday. Drier weather is \nexpected and cooler weather will break the warming trend for the\nend of the weekend. The area will likely become settled after \nSaturday as mid and upper level heights build slowly behind the \naforementioned cold frontal boundary into Sunday. Mid to upper \nlevel high pressure will reinforce settled weather going into \nthe new work week.",
